#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# probability

#% text
# `probability(c, x, d)` gives the probability that the condition `c` on `x` holds when `x` has the distribution `d`. The condition compares `x` with a bound (`x < b`, `b <= x`, `x == b`, ...) or is the conjunction of a lower and an upper bound. For the discrete families (binomial, Poisson, geometric) the bounds are rounded to the integers they allow.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
probability(x > 1, x, exponential_distribution(2))
#% output
#= exp(-2)

#% cell
probability(mu - s < x and x < mu + s, x, normal_distribution(mu, s))
#% output
#= erf((1/2)*2^(1/2))

#% cell
probability(x == 3, x, poisson_distribution(2))
#% output
#= 4/(3*exp(2))

#% cell
probability(x >= 90, x, poisson_distribution(100.))
#% output
#= 0.8536538253012671

